 like the day before that  game Jam started and quickly released it  just to make sure we could release  something and that game was called  fragile but we won't talk about that  however even though we were able to  physically ship a product it was far  from what I would consider like the  proper way to distribute games in  general I mean if we just take a look at  what Forest looked like when you  downloaded it so we had a launcher that  was pretty cool we could set our  settings we could hit play but if you  actually go into the directory this kind  of directory here is pretty much all of  like the raw assets and project files so  imagine that you've made a game in unity  but instead of like building it into  like a package and shipping it like as  an executable you kind of just ship the  unity project that's basically what we  did at that point in time if we look at  this a little bit more closely we can  see we have things like asimp here which  is like a 3D model importer which is not  generally something you would need in a  runtime of a game engine because you  should be using your own 3D model format  you shouldn't be using like fbx or gltf  because those are intermediate formats  but then if we go into like data we have  this assets folder and inside the assets  folder well guess what this is like all  of the raw assets like if we're going to  audio and Source we have a bunch of like  OG files here if we're going to mesh or  models we have all of these like gltf  files that we can just simply open and  here we have like this chest or whatever  all of these files are just here and  then inside scripts and binaries we also  have the main kind of C sharp dll this  isn't too much of a big deal this is how  Unity used to ship their games as well  before the whole il-2 CPP thing but in  general you can see how like I mean all  of the scenes for example like village.h  scene if we just open that up in Visual  Studio code you'll see that like I mean  it's all here 